WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.790
Fossil assemblages change systematically upward through many rock sequences.

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:07.290
This observed pattern is faunal succession.

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:10.790
First establish which fossils occur in older and younger beds;

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:15.190
then compare sites. Local environmental changes also affect which organisms are

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:19.330
preserved, so one missing fossil does not automatically demonstrate a worldwide

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:19.930
extinction.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:25.030
Precambrian time makes up nearly ninety percent of Earth history and

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:29.430
includes early simple life. The Paleozoic records diverse marine life and

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:33.750
colonization of land. Dinosaurs characterize much of the Mesozoic, while mammals

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.190
diversify prominently in the Cenozoic.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:39.470
These broad summaries do not mean each group first appeared exactly

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:40.830
at its named boundary.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:46.370
A population depends on food, water, habitat and interactions with other

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:50.170
species. Climate change or loss of a required partner can alter

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:55.450
survival. Reproductive strategy and adaptability affect how populations respond.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:59.510
Follow the changing habitat and population in this illustrative model;

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:02.250
its numbers are not measured extinction data.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:08.130
Competition can occur within a species or between species sharing scarce

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:12.590
resources. Predator and prey populations also depend on one another.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:16.210
A specialist that relies on one food source is vulnerable when

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:20.170
that source disappears. No organism changes because it decides to meet

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:24.330
a need; population changes must be explained through biological processes.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.190
Documented fossil changes are observations.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:32.210
Natural selection is an explanatory mechanism for evolutionary change.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:36.730
The textbook also discusses punctuated equilibrium, in which long intervals of

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:40.050
relative stability alternate with comparatively rapid change.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:45.250
Rapid here means geological time, not an individual organism transforming instantly.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.130
Keep the evidence, proposed mechanism and tempo distinct.
